WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.136
From retrieved passages to an answer you can defend.

2
00:00:03.416 --> 00:00:06.616
Weeks 1 to 3 got the right passages into your hands.

3
00:00:06.896 --> 00:00:12.037
This week is about what the model does with them, and how you find out whether it did it honestly.

4
00:00:12.317 --> 00:00:17.053
The generation step looks easy: paste the passages above the question and ask.

5
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:23.968
In practice this is where a RAG system turns good retrieval into a confident sentence the sources don’t say.

6
00:00:24.248 --> 00:00:26.467
A grounded answer has three properties.

7
00:00:26.747 --> 00:00:32.272
Every factual statement comes from the provided passages, not from the model’s general knowledge.

8
00:00:32.552 --> 00:00:35.027
Every statement points at the passage it came from.

9
00:00:35.307 --> 00:00:40.299
And when the passages don’t answer the question, the system says so instead of filling the gap.

10
00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:43.053
The first two are what citations are for.

11
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:49.136
The third is abstention, which lesson 11 treats as a design feature rather than a failure.

12
00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:52.808
Hold yourself to a stricter standard than “the answer looks right.

13
00:00:53.088 --> 00:00:58.720
” In a real team, the person who asks your HR bot about parental leave will act on the answer.

14
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.733
The citation is what lets them, or you, check it in ten seconds.

15
00:01:03.013 --> 00:01:05.125
If you can’t check it, you can’t defend it.
